The runtime dependency declaration does not match the code.

What the code does:
- lib/WWW/ARDB/Cache.pm, lib/WWW/ARDB/Result/Item.pm, lib/WWW/ARDB/Result/Quest.pm and lib/WWW/ARDB/Result/ArcEnemy.pm all `use Types::Standard qw( Str Int Num ArrayRef HashRef Maybe )` and use those types in the `has` declarations.
- The cpanfile declares `Type::Tiny` instead. Types::Standard is a thin layer over Type::Tiny and is a separate distribution, so the declared dependency does not pull in what the code loads.

Consequence: an install that trusts the cpanfile gets Type::Tiny but not Types::Standard, and every result object construction dies with "Can't locate Types/Standard.pm".

What to do:
1. In the cpanfile, replace the `Type::Tiny` entry with `Types::Standard` and keep the alphabetical ordering of the runtime block.
2. Check the whole cpanfile against the actual `use` statements in lib/ and bin/ — Moo, LWP::UserAgent, JSON::MaybeXS, HTTP::Request, URI, Path::Tiny, Digest::MD5, namespace::clean, MooX::Cmd, MooX::Options are the runtime set; Test::More and Capture::Tiny belong under `on test`.
3. Do not pin a version where none is required; never write a version of '0'.
4. Add a regression guard so this cannot drift again: a test that reads the cpanfile and asserts that every distribution named by a `use` statement in lib/ and bin/ is declared. Keep it simple and offline.

Report the diff of the cpanfile and the new test; a `Changes` entry is not needed because the dependency declaration was wrong, not the behaviour.

